1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:06,090
Have you ever joined a cloud environment where someone accidentally

2
00:00:06,090 --> 00:00:10,960
turned on a risky feature like enabling the external IPs on a

3
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:15,399
sensitive VM and nobody noticed until it was too late? That's where

4
00:00:15,399 --> 00:00:17,719
Google Cloud organization policy can help.

5
00:00:17,799 --> 00:00:19,479
Let's just go to the Google Cloud console.

6
00:00:19,950 --> 00:00:21,430
I'm inside the Google Cloud console.

7
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:24,340
On the top, I have selected the organization.

8
00:00:24,520 --> 00:00:25,360
That's important.

9
00:00:25,520 --> 00:00:29,549
These policies apply downward from where you set them.

10
00:00:29,799 --> 00:00:34,923
Now let's go to the IAM and go to the Organization policies for your entire

11
00:00:34,923 --> 00:00:39,022
cloud environment. If IAM can answer who can do what,

12
00:00:39,423 --> 00:00:43,263
then organization policy answer what is allowed to happen. And

13
00:00:43,263 --> 00:00:46,382
the magic is once a policy is enforced here,

14
00:00:46,502 --> 00:00:48,493
every folder, every project,

15
00:00:48,662 --> 00:00:53,103
and every resource inherits it unless explicitly overridden.

16
00:00:53,283 --> 00:00:56,202
Serial port access is useful for debugging,

17
00:00:56,542 --> 00:01:01,002
but in the production it can expose sensitive information.

18
00:01:01,222 --> 00:01:06,236
So to avoid that, let's disable that. In the filter, let me search constraint,

19
00:01:06,325 --> 00:01:10,886
and constraint is compute.disableSerialPortAccess.

20
00:01:11,125 --> 00:01:13,566
Now right now you can see that it is Inactive.

21
00:01:13,646 --> 00:01:16,945
I can click on these three dots and say Edit policy,

22
00:01:17,085 --> 00:01:23,246
and I switch it from inheritance to override parent's policy and say Add a rule,

23
00:01:23,356 --> 00:01:28,166
and I'm saying that Enforcement is On and say Set policy.

24
00:01:28,206 --> 00:01:30,585
Now, no VM in the organization,

25
00:01:31,005 --> 00:01:35,989
current or future, can enable serial port access unless a

26
00:01:35,989 --> 00:01:38,930
folder or a project explicitly overrides it.

27
00:01:39,069 --> 00:01:43,569
This is one of the first policies many security teams enforce

28
00:01:43,910 --> 00:01:47,910
because it prevents accidental exposure of logs,

29
00:01:47,949 --> 00:01:50,099
commands, RVM internals.

30
00:01:50,349 --> 00:01:54,660
That's how you can keep on adding more and more enforcements.

31
00:01:54,860 --> 00:02:00,110
For example, from here you can put an enforcement to the location constraint,

32
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:02,339
to the constraint for external IP creation,

33
00:02:02,580 --> 00:02:05,440
service account creation, and many more.

34
00:02:05,580 --> 00:02:11,038
So this will automatically drill down to the projects and the applications.
